Non-software licenses in dependency source distributions
---------------------------------------------------------
Some dependencies ship test fixtures under non-software licenses inside their
source distribution (sdist) only. When a compatible wheel exists, uv installs
it instead, and the wheel leaves these files out, so they do not reach an
environment or an image. A package built from its sdist (no wheel for the
platform, or a `no-binary`/`no-build` setting) needs a fresh check. License
scanners that read the sdist still report these files.

  - markdown-it-py — MIT (the code, in the wheel and the sdist). Its sdist
                     also ships the CommonMark specification text as a test
                     fixture (`tests/test_cmark_spec/spec.md` and a rendered
                     copy), licensed CC-BY-SA-4.0. The package ships a
                     universal wheel. Dev and build groups only, transitive,
                     via modal and rich; not in the bot image.

System packages in the Docker image
------------------------------------
The Dockerfile installs additional system packages into the image with
`apk add` (see the Dockerfile):

  - ffmpeg — GPL-2.0-or-later AND LGPL-2.1-or-later. Alpine builds it with
    `--enable-gpl --enable-version3`, so the shipped binary is GPL-licensed.
    It is invoked as a separate executable (a subprocess) — both by yt-dlp's
    postprocessors and directly by this project's own audio-compression code
    (see `src/utils.py`). Invoking a separate program this way is mere
    aggregation — it does NOT make this project's own code a derivative work of
    ffmpeg. Distributing an image that contains the ffmpeg binary does, however,
    carry ffmpeg's own GPL obligations for that binary.
  - deno — MIT (notice retention only).

The base image (alpine) also contains its own operating-system
packages (e.g. BusyBox, GPL-2.0-only) under their respective licenses, as any
Linux distribution image does. The same holds for the Dockerfile's separate
`migrate` stage, which is built on debian (slim) and installs no extra system
packages. compose.yaml builds that stage on every run, and its image carries
the whole `build` dependency group (including psycopg2-binary, LGPL), so the
obligations above apply to it too if it is ever distributed.

Local development environment (pixi)
-------------------------------------
The optional pixi environment (see the README) is for local development only
and is never packaged or distributed. It fetches its own ffmpeg build from
conda-forge — a different binary than the one Alpine installs into the Docker
image above. conda-forge publishes both `gpl_*` (GPL-2.0-or-later) and `lgpl_*`
variants, and `pyproject.toml` pins no variant, so assume the GPL one; either
way its obligations are dormant under the source-only distribution model
below. The repository holds only the pixi configuration in `pyproject.toml`
(`[tool.pixi.*]`), which names packages and version ranges, not the binaries
themselves. `pixi.lock` is gitignored and generated locally, so nothing of that
ffmpeg build is redistributed.

